/*
  Dhruva Burande — Anthology Hub poem archive
  Poems are stored outside index.html and grouped at runtime by collection.
*/
const POEMS_ARCHIVE = [
  { id: "WBH-001", title: "A Cup of Coffee Perhaps", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Absurdist / Visceral", language: "English", excerpt: "Blood.\nA puddle congealing, thick as thought,\nClotting in seconds like halted purpose...", content: `A CUP OF COFFEE PERHAPS\n\nBlood.\nA puddle congealing, thick as thought,\nClotting in seconds like halted purpose,\nWarming the cold air as if to mock\nThe chill that danced along his cracking lips.\nHis breath –\njagged In, out, in –out.\nPleading, but soundless.\nA noise that exists only between two dying synapses,\nAnd dies before it reaches his throat.\n\nHe —\nLooks up.\nEyes wide, irises dilating, searching for mercy\nIn a universe deaf to prayers.\nSlick red sliding between ribs,\nSlicker than the lies we tell dying men.\nThe air smells like rust and betrayal.\nMetallic.\nThe taste of life leaving too soon.\n\nAnd him.\nHim —\nStanding.\nSmiling.\nLaughing.\n\nLaughter that gnaws at the edges of sanity,\nClawing, tearing at the delicate skin of reason,\nPeeling back the flesh where morality once lived.\nA laugh, choking on itself,\nSputtering out like a dying flame\nThat refuses to die.\nWhy?\nWhy the laughter?\nWhy does the absurd plant its roots in this moment,\nWatered by blood and silence?\nWhy does he —\nLaugh?\nLaugh.\nLaugh?!\n\nNo reason.\nA joke told by a mute god\nTo a deaf creation.\nThe universe collapsing in on itself,\nLike lungs punctured by irony.\nLaughter, filling the spaces\nWhere prayers disintegrate.\nMocking the symmetry of despair.\n\nHe tries to stop.\nThe laughter bubbles,\nLike vomit rising in the throat.\nThe more he tries, the more it fights.\nA rebellion of the unconscious.\nSisyphus never laughed at the rock,\nBut maybe he should have.\n\nOne must imagine Sisyphys happy,\nThe echo slithers through his skull\nThough the boulder crushed his bones long ago...\n\nDies.\nDies.\nBlood.\nStill warm.\nStill laughing.\nBut then —\nSomething breaks.\nA hairline fracture running along the spine of the moment.\nA crack.\nBarely audible, like a gasp lost in a crowded room.\nThe laughter twists,\nCurves inward,\nBuckles under its own weight.\n\nEyes.\nStaring back at him.\nEmpty now.\nVoid of accusation,\nOf hope,\nOf meaning.\nA corpse is an honest thing —\nIt tells no lies.\nOnly silence.\nAnd he —\nStands there.\nStill smiling.\nBut something is wrong.\n\nThe laughter turns sour.\nCurdles in his throat.\nA knot.\nA sickness.\nHe chokes on it.\nTries to swallow,\nBut it refuses to go down.\n\nAnd the tears —\nUninvited, unwelcomed —\nBegin to fall.\nHot and saline,\nLike regret dissolving on his skin.\nHis body convulses,\nLaughing.\nCrying.\nLaughing.\n\nBegging.\nTo stop.\nTo anyone.\nTo anything.\nTo a god he no longer believes in.\nTo the void that answers with silence.\nTo himself.\n\nStop.\nBut there’s no one to stop.\nNo voice to answer.\nNo hand to hold.\n\nI can’t go on.\nI’ll go on\n\nThe phrase crawls up his throat, \nUnspoken like a parasite\nFeeding on the last embers of will\n\nThe loop tightens.\nLaughter suffocating tears,\nTears drowning laughter.\nCircling, spiraling,\nA snake devouring its own tail,\nTeeth piercing flesh,\nBlood feeding the cycle.\nAnd still —\nHe laughs.\nAnd still —\nHe cries.\n\nNo escape.\n\nNothing to be done.\nBecause how do you escape\nFrom the joke\nTell me how?\n\nAnd as he watches\nThe blood congeals,\nThe body cools,\nThe laughter faints\nIn his throat.` },
  { id: "WBH-002", title: "Ash Arithmetic and the Axe", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Structural / Existential", language: "English", excerpt: "Numbers.\n(Always numbers.)\nIn the margin of morning’s ledger –", content: `ASH ARITHMETIC AND THE AXE\n\nI.\nNumbers.\n(Always numbers.)\nIn the margin of morning’s ledger –\nI scribble breaths,\nExhale in algebra,\nTraded vertebrae for coin.\nThey called it gold.\nAsk for the feel of brass in my palm,\nFor ledgers with lines fat enough to trap\nthe names of those who eat\nwhat I earn.\nWrite hunger in fractions.\nI do not bow.\nI tally.\nSalt fell upward in the hourglass.\nNo one noticed.\nThe plough kept turning,\nthough the field was already sown\nwith hands.\nThe chimney smokes in silence.\nHe does not knock.\nHe never did.\nThe door opens for those\nwho bring fire\nwrapped in numbers.\nGifts in joys –\nparceled in paycheques,\nwrapped in ruptured wrists,\nlabelled: “Unseen but Needed.”\nWhat one needs anyways\nOnly the pulse of addition.\nThey do not see –\n(but that is the point of seeing)\na man is a scaffolding of "have to"s.\nNever the roof.\nOnly the beams.\nOnly the burn.\n\nII.\nOnce –\nA cracked spine like walnut shells\nFed them comfort.\nOnce –\nEyes wept between invoices.\nThey speak in baubles and kindness\nin small coin of the mouth.\nOnce –\nGave away absence \nPresence spent\nat the furnace of necessity.\nNot candles but kilns.\nWhat is kindness if not consistency\nforged into hours\nof unmade sleep?\nAnd still I build.\nAlways.\nOnly.\n\nIII.\nPenance has no audience.\nOnly mirrors that lie.\nConstruction of esteem with calloused blueprints.\nWith a hand that shakes but does not drop the hammer.\nWith the grin of a furnace.\nWith a back that knows no Sunday.\nA creature of structure.\nA blacksmith of want.\nEven my rest is functional.\nEven my joy, transferable.\nThey sit in my skeleton and\ncall it shelter.\nThey call it unnecessary.\nUnseen, the scaffolding sighs.\n\nIV.\nBut O–O  when the ledgers close!\nWhen the last child smiles full\non a belly, filled—\nI will vanish.\nAt Pine smokes and timbers\nWhere the clatter stops,\nWhere breath is not a currency\nbut a craft.\nWhere the dog is no witness\nbut a companion.\nWhere the cat judges only\nin metaphors.\nWhere nails are for cabins, not coffins.\n\nV.\nNorth,\nThere is bread.\nThere is tea.\nThere is wood\nstacked like prayer.\nAnd most importantly\nThere is no applause.\nI will not rest.\nI’ll return\nto the first number,\nbefore division.\nBefore debt.\nThe original integer.\nLet the math be done.\nLet the fire take the page.` },
  { id: "WBH-003", title: "Bent Ballad of a Broken Brute", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Raw / Melancholic", language: "English", excerpt: "Beside me a bad man broke, they bawled.\nLaying, fading, blemishing, reappearing, laying...", content: `BENT BALLAD OF A BROKEN BRUTE\n\nBeside me a bad man broke, they bawled.\nLaying, fading, blemishing, reappearing, laying,\nHis stout, apple neck resting,\nCoarse, curly, crimpled hair unevened by the mass of his own mind,\nJust laying, fading, blurring, then reappearing again.\nThe bad man broke, they bombarded.\nOn his buttocks, bore he, his bubble bellied body,\nFull of brisky beard that he bore on his blackish brown skin\nThat so conveniently covered his coarse lips\nDried of aspired utterance –whose only mention astounded his earthly body.\nThe bad man broke, they so babblingly babbled.\nHis mind so scarily scornful, they swore,\nSo much so that a simple singular substance of that skull can hide thousand silver synapses,\nThat can burn the station down to ashes.\nThe bad man broke, they breathed.\nThe nudity of his body covered that of his soul,\n\nThe Man at the Station-\nA body, brittle, caked in black,\nash or skin or time—who knows?\nBent at the edge where the platform ends,\nwhere no one looks, where no one stops.\nHis lips, black like the rest of him,\ncracked, maybe speaking, maybe not.\nA whisper too dry to break the air,\na name, a plea—forgotten before it left.\nCommuters pass, eyes fixed ahead,\nschedules, hands in pockets, moving, moving.\nNo space for the man who does not belong\nto time, to hunger, to the need to be seen.\n\nIf he falls, the train will take him.\nIf he stands, the wind will not.\nIf he breathes, it is out of habit.\nIf he dies, the day will go on.\n\nThe bad man broke, they said\nWhy?\nNone asked.\nI will let him sin on the pedestal of the evil\nOr what you call today- God's home\nAnd let him weep his final tear,\nGasp his final breath,\nHis final gaze to strip the world bare,\nHis final silence speak what we never will.\nLet him cleanse it all—the filth, the faith, the forgetting.\nLet him cleanse the gutters and gods alike,\nLet him cleanse me, who stood, who watched, who said nothing.` },
  { id: "WBH-004", title: "Bruised (For If I Told You That I Love You)", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Introspective / Vulnerable", language: "English", excerpt: "For if I told you that I love you\nAnywhere but in my poetry,\nI know my pen\nWould Slip Away...", content: `BRUISED\n\nFor if I told you that I love you\nAnywhere but in my poetry,\nI know my pen\nWould Slip Away,\nAnd it would too\nGive you the power to walk away,\nBut no, my dear,\nSo, for now I will write\nFor not manifesting my love\nAnd never for denying it.\nI write implicit poetry\nWords inside me that I burry,\nFor I have mastered the art of concealing\nThrough experience, the techniques of disguising\nOptical illusion overlaid on a lucid painting.\n\nThe sourness in my eye\nVeiled within a mimic smile\nLips dyed in radiant color so they occur\nBut a dull abstract as u look closer.\nA weightless body floating between time & space\nA glitch in a cosmos, no gravity in my case;\nMurky blood from a ruptured spleen,\nA conversation in the dark with the monsters I’ve seen.\nHaunted by the spoken but never meant\nA feign genesis from which I lack Kent,\nDevils resembling a facsimile saint\nWords with their actions they never acquaint.\n\nTook a lesson from the repeated voice\nTo be stuck or to evolve is my choice,\nFading in the past or living in the present\nCherishing the mistakes or frowning at the moment.\nSo here I’m writing\nWith all I got embracing,\nInscribing my pain with the ink from my heart\nPalms bruised so I won’t fall apart;\nTo the scheme of world to the pit of lie\nI might be here but your deceits I won’t buy,\nSo, to infect me with your diseases\nPlease don’t try\nOver losing this attitude\nI would rather choose to die.\nAs an authentic bruised version of me\nWithout a single manipulation there is to be.\n\nSo, for now again\nI’ll let my fountain pen drain,\nAnd spill its blood out\nOn this half-wrenched paper,\nAnd scribble the words for no doubt\nAnd create an art ‘till the burning piece taper.\nAnd pierce my heart\nAnd ache my head till its bursts\nAnd till my lung stifle\nAnd my hands go weak\nAnd my eyes drench\nAnd my ears bleed\nAnd my nose drop\nAnd my paper withers…\nI will then drown out the wrapped\nInto the ashbin\nUntil it soaks all the scrap\nAnd then ask for something\nThat’s long gone into the dark iniquity\nBut lasts in my soul till eternity.` },
  { id: "WBH-005", title: "Fresh Cut", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Visceral / Dark", language: "English", excerpt: "Begin with the night, shall we?\nProwling to the preyed prey\nThat prays me to stay away...", content: `FRESH CUT\n\nBegin with the night, shall we?\nProwling to the preyed prey\nThat prays me to stay away\n‘How can I?’ I ask in dismay\nIt retreats once, I forge ahead twice\nIt covets the melting sun on a grain of rice\nPeers the fading sunlight as it lifts its chest\nHunting for aid, hapless and helpless\nI carve a cut and offer it a slice \nIt glances in glee as I gulp its glimpse\nI scoop the second with a spoon and compromise\nThe same as I satisfy her whims.\n\nThree spoons of crimson did the work\nBut how could I leave it unburned \nAs the searing sunlight pleasures its particle\nI poach another part with my cunning cuticles.\nAnother part.\nA piece that pulses\nEven in my hand.\nHer body betrays her,\nPleading silently, subtly,\nAs if blood can bargain.\n\nI grated its surface as it sunk its spread\nUntil t’was all rose, vermillion and red\nJust to veer it around and turn her head\nRepeating everything again, again and again.\n	\nLike peeling fruit.\nBut fruit doesn’t squirm. \nFruit doesn’t flinch. \nShe does.\n\nNow t’was time for a fresh cut \nFrom the bottom, sides and a little inside\nShe guards every touch, even from my tender guide, \nFor within those depths, my presence she'd rather hide\nI sprinkle some salt and sesame in her guts\nSeasoning her flavour with just my touch.\n\nLook how happy and rosy she looks\nAll blushing with her body, right on my hook.\nSalt.\nSesame.\nI sprinkle it slow.\nSeasoning her insides.\nSalt seeps into wounds,\nThe sesame?\nFor the scent.\nBecause even death deserves flavor.\n\nBut not yet.\nNot done.\nHer skin cools,\nBut I’m not done.\nA final flick.\nA kiss of steel on her throat,\nSoft, seductive, swift.\nA slit —\nBarely there,\nBut enough to hush\nThe last whimper.\n\n“Happy now?”\nI ask.` },
  { id: "WBH-006", title: "God Who Bleeds", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Mythological / Chaotic", language: "English", excerpt: "One foot to quietus,\nRight beyond some tender cuts and animal guts,\nOther on some spilled wine...", content: `God Who Bleeds\n\nOne foot to quietus, \nRight beyond some tender cuts and animal guts, \nOther on some spilled wine, \nStand I, erect beneath a flipped dime.\n\nStand I, might well drown and sink true down, yeah, amid the red ocean floors, \nMy soldiers sprawled in blood and gore, aye, blood and gore.\n\nSwimming through their weightless flesh, \nA Tsade (righteous person) then a Qoph (Profanity) \nand a letdown Resh (Head), \nCame forth I, against my self-carved mess.\n\nSee how no one is guilty, see how no one is to blame, \nNo one’s at fault, it’s all in their names.\n\nTwenty-one now, a fiasco that’s drawn to my feet, again, on this murky street, \nI step up on the garden, half complete.\n\nI step up to see the light growing darker, fiercer, and a little more blurred, \nLike the beginning of singularity, or just an anomaly, \nMaybe just some excessive vanity, \nAn entity that was too lonely to even exist, \nBut whatever that less angelic and more demonic pretend was, \nI decided, it was God.\n\nI saw in the stalls of pearls the heavenly host, \nGentle as down and averted from all the hurts, \n“Dies Irae [day of wrath]” she sang and I could smell\nThe dead white phosphorous of the sacred hearts.\n\n“God?”, I mumbled \nAnd the lady, as she seemed a little fumbled, said, \n“Back to your comrades you want to go eh?\nYour life hasn’t been so meaningless they say \nAnd the rest shall be kept for the judgment day.”\n\nJudgment day?\nHuh!\nIs that what you call a system failure today?\nWhere the codes are unwritten, the records missing\nAnd the executioner is a blind man, clicking away.\n\nI stepped up to see the throne of shards,\nGuarded by a flock of faceless birds,\n“Elohi, Elohi, [My God, My God]” I cried out loud,\nBut the sky was just a bruised, purple shroud.\nWhat happened to the layout of your soldiers eh?\n\nI, the parent of the earth, \nCreator of all the births, \nReason behind your mirth, \nA heathen god where the word of Ymir is not read,\nWhere the dead come to seek solitude,\nI am the one they come praying to.\n\nMy will to bless you with salvation, \nBy not letting you witness the annihilation, \nOf those you call your worthy fellows,\nWho’ll reside here in the further few moments, \nAfter getting crushed like rodents, have diminished.\n\nNow, may the black sun cast psycho-destructive shadows over your bloody skies today.\n\nAnd as you rush to the house of god to find your long-lost solace, \nMay you cross paths with a faceless man \nWhose morality is as black as the pages you burnt from the book of your life.\n\nAnd as the shadows of your fallen comrades dance over the drowning skies, \nThe antichrist shall mutilate your fragile system.\n\nWhen Bartzabel (God of Jupiter) heeds your pleas and your cries, \nSatan shall become a man whose verses lack rhythm-...”\n“Urusendayo, Is that so?\n\nEven if we’re doomed with unforgettable woe \nAnd be welcomed at purgatory’s door, \nAt Paradis, we now need a god who bleeds, \nWhose wounds, are not the end of Eternity.\n\nThis wall, hiding the truth, will fall. \nAnd as we call Ymir to bless us the power to fight the 17-meter-tall damn monkey, \nYou’ll recall the words \nAnd witness the stall I made.\n\nSo many times, I thought death will be much easier \nAnd now...` },
  { id: "WBH-007", title: "Her", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Celestial / Mournful", language: "English", excerpt: "Time heretofore that once lived\nWhen the heavens cracked and bowed to the distant horizon...", content: `HER\n\nTime heretofore that once lived\nWhen the heavens cracked and bowed to the distant horizon\nWhere wisps of white mist lay upon the roof\nAnd a blue haze veiled over, aloof\nThere studded, a golden orb, radiant and warm\nReigned the mornings but feared the nights' embrace;\nDelayed greets the pale prince, clad in white\nNever needed to be shielded from prying eyes and reaching hands\nBut from the voids within\nEnveloped by a black cardigan\nClouded with tinkling holes \nThose that wake the substance of life\nIn the mourning as well as praying homes.\n\nSomewhere,\nMe had taken birth.\nA weary smile trailed like a fading comet's light\nEyes that brought disgrace to sunshine\nA celestial symphony, where dreams align\nShe was a temporary distress, a permanent delight\nArms that could screen a million fireflies\nAnd another billion Frostbites\nWithout a whimper\nHer skin, soft as petals kissed by dawn's first light\nRadiating a warmth that cradled the night\nHer hair, a cascade of cascading silk threads\nLike a flowing river, it gently spread\nThe vessel, life’s sacred art\nSmiled at her realistic design.\n\nThere, On the other side,\nA mother rejoiced her birth.` },
  { id: "WBH-008", title: "I Have No Poem for You", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Reckless / Raw", language: "English", excerpt: "I have no poem for you\nFor poems aren’t my friends anymore.\nMy heart is no longer a sanctum of those phrases.", content: `I Have No Poem for You\n\nI have no poem for you\nFor poems aren’t my friends anymore.\nMy heart is no longer a sanctum of those phrases.\nMy emotions no longer birth poetry.\nMy sentiments no longer take the shape of words.\nPoems aren’t my mirror anymore\nFor I am broken as a glass full of cracks.\nYou are the complexity of my pen.\nMy pen knows you aren’t a deceit.\nMy mind is a labyrinth which has now parted me into seventeen\nAnd with time\nSixteen of them might turn against me\nBut the only part that shall stay constant\nIs the one that belongs to you.\n\nThe paper is a garden, and my pen is a plough\nThe flowers in the garden refuse being poetic\nThey do not ooze gentleness and beauty\nThey are fierce, not fearful.\nI no longer mould my emotions to make them comprehensible\nThey are reckless and unapologetic\nThey no longer fear judgment\nThat what seems to be eternal love?\nI am afraid it is evanescent.\n\nWhy are we on different ends of the poem?\nWhy are we stuck here?\nThis isn’t our home.\nMy poems have taken a new form now\nMy words are no longer pleasant\nBut are ferocious\nYou might try to compose a song out of them\nBut I’m afraid that you’ll lack rhythm.\n\nI am on a journey to find my true self\nYou are the path I take and the shelter I seek.\nConversations often create confusions,\nLet it be.\nLet’s not waste time looking for clarity\nAll I can offer you is me.\nAlthough I have lost the control over myself\nAnd you?\nYou aren’t a stranger to me either\n\nI wanna see you\n‘Cause, I want to have a glimpse of your morning face one more time\nWould write a book on eternal beauty then.\nI would like to carve the traces of your touch on my bones\nI would like if it hurts\n‘Cause that’s when I would write, right?\nThat would be the time when my pen would bleed\nAnd the bloodstains on the paper would shape itself \nIn the story we shared.\n\nI know you’ll be there, you always were\nYou were always careful\nNot to step on the boundaries\nWhich you created for us both.\nYeah, you make sure to be there if I sobbed\nTo console me, but never up close.\nWould you mind if I ask you to stay longer this time?\nThat would nullify the difference.\nIt’s amazing how these simple words mean so much\n“Cause I’m sure they are the ones you yearn to hear\nI have no need for poetry if I have your touch\nBut then these words won’t make a way out to your ears.\n\nI would like to enjoy every moment of your presence from now on.\nI’m aware that I’ve never been enough\nBut I am learning to be fine with it.` },
  { id: "WBH-009", title: "I Pray for You, God", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Indictment / Modernist", language: "English", excerpt: "I.\nNo cathedral.\nOnly a footpath glued with spit,\na man asleep on cardboard...", content: `I Pray for You, God\n\nI.\nNo cathedral.\nOnly a footpath\nglued with spit,\na man asleep on cardboard\nthat smells like yesterday’s rain\nand tomorrow’s rot.\nFlies rehearse resurrection\non his eyelids.\n\nI refuse that filing cabinet.\nI refuse the holy hush,\nthe velvet-mouthed theodicy\nthat licks wounds\nonly to keep them open.\nCall it “trial.”\nCall it “test.”\nCall it “necessary evil.”\nCall it dialectic,\nantithesis, synthesis—\n\nSomewhere\na child counts ribs\nlike mistakes,\none-two-too-many,\nwhile the sky practices\nbeing blue\nwith no intention behind it.\nTell the child with ribs like parentheses\nthat starvation is an argument\nthat must finish itself.\n\nIf you are watching,\nyou have a strong stomach,\nyou bastard.\n\nII.\nI have seen faith\nbow its spine into a question mark\nin front of bread.\nI see a woman\nscooping food from a drain\nwith fingers blistered into facts.\nNo parable fits her mouth.\nNo proverb feeds her back.\n\nMen kneel\nnot in prayer\nbut in negotiation\nwith sewage luck.\nWomen barter silence\nfor safety that never arrives.\n\nThere’s a man\ntalking to traffic,\narguing with red lights,\nhis thoughts unstitched,\nhis name misplaced\nbetween shock and schizophrenia.\nOnce, he wanted to build bridges.\nNow he is one—\neveryone walks over him\nand calls it progress.\n\nIf this is part of a plan,\nit’s written in disappearing ink\nand paid for in screams.\nTell me again\nhow suffering refines.\nTell me again\nhow pain polishes the soul\nlike its silverware\nand not a knife\nleft inside the body.\n\nIII.\nThey tell me: Without darkness, how would you know light?\n\nA child laughs in a ward,\nher leg amputated by an incoming shell.\nThe shrapnel is secular.\nIt doesn't choose by scripture.\nIs she the shadow that makes your profile sharp?\nIs her gangrene necessary for your glory?\n\nKeep your light.\nGive her back the knee.\nGive her back the uncomplicated dirt of the playground.\nI do not need a contrast to know joy.\nI do not need a graveyard to love the grass.\n\nYour arithmetic is cruel.\nYour balance sheets are heavy with marrow.\n\nIV.\nLet us pray for you, God.\n\nBecause you must be terrified of your own memory.\nYou must look down at this spinning asylum,\nthis wet, frantic ball of dust,\nand wish you had stayed asleep in the void.\nYou must hear the choirs and feel sick.\nThe praise must sound like mockery.\nThe hallelujahs must burn your ears like lye.\n\nHow do you sit still through the oncology clinics?\nHow do you ignore the sound of teeth grinding in sleep?\nYou must be so lonely,\ntrapped in your perfection,\nunable to apologize because an absolute cannot bleed.\n\nSo I pray for your release.\nI pray for your end.\nMay you be broken into small, useless things.\nA pebble.\nA dry leaf.\nA drop of grease on a mechanic's sleeve.\nSomething that doesn't carry the weight of a world.\n\nV.\nThey say you have no beginning and no end.\nWhat a terrible sentence.\n\nEven the worst storm runs out of water.\nEven the longest night runs out of shadow.\nBut you—you are stuck with yourself forever.\nNo exit.\nNo resignation.\nNo suicide possible for eternity.\n\nYou are the ultimate prisoner.\nThe walls of your cell are made of stars.\nAnd we—we are just the graffiti you scratch on the plaster\nto pass the time.\n\nVI.\nI leave the incense unlit.\nI leave the book closed.\n\nIf you want my attention,\nspeak in the vernacular of the wound.\nDo not send a prophet with clean teeth.\nDo not send an angel with ironed wings.\n\nCome down into the damp.\nSit on the cardboard.\nSmell the rot.\nLet the flies land on you.\n\nLet’s see how your omnipotence handles the fever.\nLet’s see if your majesty can survive the queue.\nBut you hide well.\n\nBehind words like process, patience, purpose. \nBehind philosophers who disinfect suffering with grammar.\nThe scales are snapped.\nThe hands are missing.\nThe judge never showed.\n\nI pray that if you exist you remain undiscovered, unproven, unarmed with legitimacy,\nStay theoretical.\nStay hidden behind footnotes and fog.\nStay rumor.\nStay shadow.\nStay hypothetical.\n\nDo not step into evidence like a criminal returning to the scene with excuses rehearsed.\nI do not want reconciliation.\nI want indictment.\nI want an answer that bleeds as much as the question.\n\nVII.\nI choose the human over the holy.\nThe wounded over the worshipped.\nThe scream over the scripture.\n\nIf meaning must be made, we will make it without blood-tithes.\nIf goodness must exist, it will be built by hands that bleed and still refuse to call the knife sacred.\n\nLet the universe be empty before it be cruel.\nLet silence reign before a tyrant wears the crown of stars.\n\nThis is not blasphemy.\nNot rebellion for fashion.\nNot anger looking for applause.\nThis is bookkeeping—cold, unromantic, exact.\nColumns of amputations.\nRows of hunger.\nMargins filled with names that never reached full syllables.\n\nThis is a charge sheet, itemized, dated, witnessed by streets, wards, queues, ruins.\nAnd you—whether you are real or only a very old excuse—have been silent through every count.\n\nSilence is not neutrality.\nSilence is testimony.\nAnd it has been speaking against you for far too long.\n\nAnd if someday a God, You, finally answer—\nlet it be in a courtroom, not a cathedral.` },
  { id: "WBH-010", title: "Immortal Combat", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Existential / Defiant", language: "English", excerpt: "Ash—\nbone-grey sky collapsed into the lungs.\nI breathe / but not for long.", content: `IMMORTAL COMBAT\n\nAsh—\nbone-grey sky collapsed into the lungs.\nI breathe / but not for long.\nTime,\nthat thieving child\nwith wristwatch teeth—\nnips.\nTicks.\nLicks\nthe marrow from the hour.\n\nIt begins here.\nIn the knowing.\nThe slow, dull carving of it—\nhow hard it is to strive against one’s own mortality.\nNot the dying itself.\nBut the fight.\nKnowing you must fight.\nWith blunted nails and rotting grace.\n\nI walked last night\nwhere clocks forget their faces.\nGraves whisper louder\nthan prophets,\nand even gods\ncough dust in the dark.\nThey do not listen.\nThey never did.\nNot when you shout,\nnot when you whisper,\nnot when you plead in poetry.\n\nBut still—\nhow hard it is to strive against one’s own mortality.\nTo not let go.\nTo not\nunravel.\nSpine.\nStone.\nMirrorbone.\nAll the same, once winter’s grown.\n\nI cried out—\nbut the echo came\nin my father’s voice,\ncracked as a chapel bell\nin November’s frost.\nHe said:\nRun.\nBleed.\nSwim in the seed.\nThe end is the only creed.\n\nI prayed\nto broken things:\na rusted hinge,\na torn hymnbook,\na mother’s ring—\nnone replied.\nOnly the maggots nodded.\nThey understand.\nThey feast quietly,\nwithout hope,\nwithout memory.\nAnd maybe that’s peace.\n\nBut still—\nI scream.\nI burn.\nBecause I know\nhow hard it is to strive against one’s own mortality.\n\nNot death.\nBut the silence after the name fades.\n\nChildhood:\na lie painted in sunbeam.\nYouth:\na glass blade,\nsheathed in skin,\nshattered by grin.\n\nAnd Death—\nno scythe, no shadow\njust\na shrug\nin a sterile coat,\nwriting "Routine."\nNo crescendo.\nNo trumpet.\nJust the sigh of the curtain.\nThe hush after the joke.\nAnd we all nod, don’t we?\nAs if we knew—\nas if we agreed—\nbut inside,\ninside,\nwe rage\nbecause we know\nhow hard it is to strive against one’s own mortality.\n\nStill—\nI fight.\nGnash.\nSpit.\nRip through myth.\nEven in rot,\nthe soul might lift.\n\nI rage in the cellar\nwhere God’s fingers\nno longer reach.\nI rage\nat the ceiling fan's sermon—\neach spin a sin.\nEach breath a breach.\n\nThis is not dignity.\nThis is not release.\nThis is\ndragging your soul\nthrough shards of yesterday\njust to say:\nI was here.\n\nI drag my name\nthrough thorn and thought,\nchanting:\nNot yet.\nNot yet.\nNot yet.\n\nO Time—\nyou crooked priest,\nyou beg for sacrifice\nwith a smile\nfull of nails.\nYou call it sleep.\nYou call it grace.\nYou call it\nplace.\nBut I\ncall it war.\nI call it more.\nI call it what\nthe dead forgot.\n\nAnd every day\nI rise against you\nwith ink, with breath,\nwith trembling hands,\nand a truth that weighs\nmore than a grave—` },
  { id: "WBH-011", title: "October Awaits", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Nostalgic / Melancholic", language: "English", excerpt: "Five years have past; five summers, with the length\nOf five winters, once again.", content: `OCTOBER AWAITS\n\nFive years have past; five summers, with the length\nOf five winters, once again.\nOh this empty state!\nOrange meets the brown in night’s wake,\nChildren of amber, vermillion and red,\nSlowly shiver and hover to the vortex’s end;\nHow frail! How weak!\nContinuously decaying as we speak.\n\nBut\nA wind will pass tomorrow, at dawn’s break\nShambling and rumbling over the puzzle;\nPainting the town in red\nBefore it escapes the muzzle.\n\nMaybe a drop will fall\nOne down your eyes, two on the wall;\nSome through your chest\nTo the door of your lungs\nWho now crave the northern wind’s trace.\nSome lying by your feet\nThat seek solace in retreat;\nTwo on your fingertips\nThat escape by the ink that gently spills,\nFew on the rose grown on his grave\n13 on her window, still unopened.\n\nA tree had fallen then, by a wind\nA muse had failed to make a song out of him;\nOne scratch during the day\nSome more in the night,\nFrom the box where his heart lays\nTo find the keys that lead to the light,\nAnd retrieve the ascending piece, covering his eyes\nAnd open up the sky,\nTo only find a reason\nStated in a lie.\n\nThe bell, hereafter, may never ring,\nOne more chair than you’ll ever need;\nHow you’re expected to turn your face toward the city lights\nWith a shadow, lifeless, behind.\nLong has it been since he’s waned and ceased\nNow, just a remain, a monument, a ruin\nStripped off of his glories, never returned, an endless ease\nLying yellow, pale but still, human.\n\nYou loved him, all of him\nEven now, every bit\nHis bowed head, his buried face,\nAll waits, suspended\nHis autumn tree, his autumn sky,\nThe blackbird, poor fool, that sings out of season\nWho to the willows by the lake flies.\nAhh Birds!\nYou like the flights of the pigeons over him,\nHis fragments of freedom, hazard, an anagram made of flesh;\nAnd somewhere, stinging smell of burning leaves,\nHis ship has changed, but still the same.\n\nHe is there, he always was\nTurn to your phone, wait for his call,\nStand by his bed, sit on his spot,\nWear his clothes, feel his warmth,\nStroll among the flower garden, find his scent,\nCome back home, sleep in his chair.\n\nAs for the leaf that falls,\nEven October awaits\nAn unfinished poem\nBegs for a fitting closure,\nA lytic mind longs for respite,\nAn artery pines for a rupture,\nA dead cigarette yearns for another kiss,\nJust like a drowning man needs a final water intake,\nThe green pastoral landscape is to you\nMore dear, both for himself and thy sake.` },
  { id: "WBH-012", title: "Red Wood", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Spiritual / Visceral", language: "English", excerpt: "Wood.\nWounded wood. A scent that clings to His nostrils,\nSlithering into His skin, Raw.", content: `RED WOOD\n\nWood.\nWounded wood.\nA scent that clings to His nostrils,\nSlithering into His skin,\nRaw.\n\nEarthy.\nAs if Eden’s earth still clings\nTo the roots,\nWhispering sins into the sap.\nThe splinters prickle the air,\nPine, perhaps.\nCedar, conceivably.\nBut no —\nOlive wood.\n\nOld. Ominous.\nThe same olive wood that once burned\nIn His father’s temple.\nAshes to ashes.\nNow pressed against His back.\nNow nuzzling His nape,\nLike a lover lying, lingering,\nWhispering promises that rot in the marrow.\n\nHe breathes.\nDeep.\nDeeper than desire dares.\nThe scent, thick with memory,\nSweeter than wine at Cana,\nBitterer than betrayal bleeding in Gethsemane.\nIt smells of beginnings,\nOf the first fig tree that kissed the sunlight\nAnd the last branch that will break.\nThe last tree that will rot in silence.\nIt smells —\nLike home.\nLike heaven hollowed out.\nLike the place He was before He was. \nAnd beneath it all,\nThe faintest trace of sweat,\nThe scent of man.\nMortal.\nMute.\nMangled.\nFraile.\nFragile.\nFalling.\n\nAnd He wonders —\nWas the wood always wise?\nDid the sap that ran through this wood\nKnow it will one day\nDrink His blood?\nDid His Father,\nIn all His foresight,\nWhisper wicked wisdom to the sapling,\n“Stand strong, child.\nYou will bear my Son.”\nOr did He let it grow blindly,\nLet it stretch silently,\nUnaware that its limbs\nWould one day\nCradle crucifixion.\nWithout knowing\nThat it would hold God’s broken body?\n\n“Father…”\nThe word cracks, crumbles,\nLike dry wood splitting in the heat.\nBut no answer.\nNot now.\nNot yet.\n\nThe weight presses down —\nBones bending, nerves screaming,\nBut His mind drifts back\nTo that carpenter’s workshop,\nWhere Joseph’s hands guided His\nOver rough wood,\nTeaching Him to smooth the edges,\nTo make the grain obey.\n“Steady your hand, Son.”\n\nSteady.\nBut how does one steady a hand\nThat will one day\nScream with the weight\nOf a world too heavy to hold?\nHow does one breathe?\nWhen the scent of the wood\nForetells the stench of sacrifice.\n\nHe remembers.\nSawdust settling. \nThe smell.\nClinging to His skin,\nFilling the air,\nLike a prophecy waiting to wither.\nThe wood was innocent then.\nUntouched by blood.\nBut innocence rots quickly,\nDoesn’t it?\n\n“Father…”\nAgain.\nA whisper, weaker,\n“Why have You forsaken me?”\nBut silence swallows.\nBecause even silence\nKnows when to speak.\nKnows that the universe holds its breath\nWhen God speaks to Himself.\n\nHe Inhales.\nDeep.\nThe wood.\nThe scent of birth,\nThe scent of death,\nThe scent of eternity\nCollapsing into a single moment.\nAnd somewhere —\nIn the air thick with thorns and thought,\nBetween the wood and the nails,\nHe smells…\nHis Father.\nBut not the God of\nWrath and ruin.\nNo.\n\nThe quiet one.\nThis one walks.\nThe one who walks in the garden\nIn the cool of the day.\nThe one who waits.\nWaits while His Son\nSuspended between\nSky and soil,\nCaught in the tension\nOf infinity and the void.\n\n“I’m here.”\nBut the words never leave His lips.\nThey die before birth,\nDissolving in the heat,\nWhile the scent of the cross\nRemains.\n\nWood.\nPressed into His skin.\nSplinters burying themselves like memories,\nLike sins,\nLike the weight of a world\nThat doesn’t know how to carry itself.\n\nAnd still…\nHe breathes it in.\nDeeper this time.\nBecause what else can He do?\nWhen the only thing keeping Him\nSuspended between heaven and earth\nIs the same wood\nThat smells like home.\n\n“Forgive them…”\nThe words escape,\nSlipping through dried lips\nAs the scent of the cross\nBecomes the scent of eternity.\n\nBut no answer.\nNot yet.\n\nOnly wood.\nAnd silence.\n\nAnd then —\nIt happens.\nA shiver.\nBarely noticeable.\nA tremor in the air,\nAs if the world\nHolds its breath\nFor just a moment.\n\nThe scent changes.\nNo longer wood.\nNo longer earth.\nBlood.\n\nAnd in that moment —\nHe understands.\nUnderstands the joke,\nThe irony,\nThe idea of saving\nA world\nThat doesn’t know how to be saved.\n\nAnd still —\nThe scent lingers.\nFaint.\nRefusing to die.` },
  { id: "WBH-013", title: "Sermon in Shell Casings", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Reckless / Raw", language: "English", excerpt: "White. Golden.\nRed. Vest. Breath. I stand strapped...", content: `SERMON IN SHELL CASINGS\n\nWhite. Golden.\nRed. Vest.\nBreath.\nI stand strapped, ribs wrapped, monks map decades on faces of ash,\nthe line outside winds wide, tide of hands with coins that clink like cracked knuckles,\nmetal for ritual, miracle for sale.\n\nExchange—\nSlot shot stock, coin drop lock,\nCommerce in the cloister, Christ as cashier,\nCapital carved into chalice, communion commercialized,\nHoly hands turned hustlers, monks as middlemen.\nThis is no Mass but a market—\nLine-item liturgy,\nProfit as prophet.\nEvery coin buys a cartridge, every cartridge buys confession.\n\nJesus—\nA communist, comrade carpenter,\nFeeding thousands with loaves for free,\nHe’d flip the table, smash the slots,\nKick the coins, curse the clerks.\nGospel garnished with gunpowder,\nHis sermon swallowed by spreadsheets,\nHis body brokered by bankers in robes.\n\nRaw humour of it—\nBullets should be free, grace cheap,\nbut salvation sold by the round,\nmartyrdom marked up with tax,\nredemption bought wholesale,\nGod outsourced to gunmetal.\n\nCandles—\nlittle suns spun, one by one undone,\nchandelier shivers silver, slivers scatter,\nsaints stare, fresco fissures fissile,\ncracked ceiling bleeding symmetry.\n\nNoise.\nGlass shatters, bombs batter,\nbass drums hum with screams streaming,\ntechno-tomb tune, looped lunacy,\nstatic stitched with skin-scorch.\n\nI dip the bullet, water hisses, blessing blistered,\nload it, hold it, holy wholly unholy,\nmouths open, altars altered,\ntongues stretched, flesh fetched,\nwaiting wafer but taking barrel,\ncross-hand command, crucifix kiss—\n\nLeadlights still whitewashed\nclick crack flash hush.\n\nEpiphany—\nif sinner, stack, if saint, scar,\nboth bear holes whole,\nboth return retching testimonies.\n\nMartyrdom again,\nAgain,\nAgain,\nrecycled, repeated, massed, mocked, marked ‘one for four’\npile of bodies, pyramids of proof,\nsalvation sold, speculation told,\nmiracle mirrored in blood on the floor.\n\nOne coin in my pocket.\nThat one coin buys eternity,\nbut costs you the comedy of walking away.\n\nSalvation slips so easily into the slot.\nSo I hold it—\nKeep it\nKeep it warm as if it remembers every hand,\nan escape no larger than a fingernail.\nNot from greed but the grin that comes\nwhen construed—\nthe system stutters if you don’t play.\n\nWhat?\nFaith.\nFraud.\nFlesh.\nReligion.` },
  { id: "WBH-014", title: "Sweet Escape", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Celestial / Mournful", language: "English", excerpt: "Just for a moment, you see it clearly\nEver so perfect then it’s gone.", content: `SWEET ESCAPE\n\nJust for a moment, you see it clearly\nEver so perfect then it’s gone.\nJust like a sunset, you’ll never forget\nDrowning in the ever-empty horizon.\n\nYou felt like resting when the night drew in,\nBut that’s not the place for you to belong.\nThey scrape your bones right down to the skin,\nWhere one truth ends and another lie begins.\n\nYou fold into the ages and say,\n“If you gaze long into the Abyss, back into you, the Abyss sure stares.”\nThere then, you get crucified at the border of your heart\nWhere the tormented soul ties, and the mind pulls apart.\n\nLet me hold your pen, let me take your sins\nBlur the real and hum you the hymn\nI’ll put you to sleep and gulp down your pain\nLove, I’ll be your sweet escape.` },
  { id: "WBH-015", title: "Sweet Prometheus's Liver", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Mythological / Existential", language: "English", excerpt: "How far is it to peace?\nAs the loud tempest yelling rise", content: `SWEET PROMETHEUS'S LIVER\n\nHow far is it to peace?\nAs the loud tempest yelling rise\nThe gales lay caprice\nThe clouds far off the bay.\n\nBehold, on whom rests the weight of the heavens\nBoiling down in wrath, in seek of the master of the hearth\nThe heir, who yet bears no inferno leaven\nI, Prometheus, guardian of the earth.\n\nI, Prometheus,\nIn the pitch-black shade, bestowed the eye of the light\nto my children lost in spite\nA point of singularity that drifted away unfathomable reverie\nThat just seldom coincides with reality.\nA known paradox, an unknown simplicity\nAgreed, I, only to contradict\nIn the most admiring felicity.\n\nI, Prometheus,\nOn the wake of my sins\nChoose to breach the Olympian walls\nEvery time I see a mortal fall\nIn the pit of Anguish.\nI share the wheezing in their chests\nThe wake of their nights\nAnd then remember,\nThe lethargy in the innocence engulfing their wings\nThose hidden feathers that the mighty fears\nThat carry their hearts up to the sky\nJust to touch mercy with their fingertips.\n\nI, Prometheus,\nDrenched in blood that lines His fate\nAnd that which one day will touch him by a whisker\nJust in another shape\nJust in another form\nBut the tint will never change.\nOver and Over.\nZeus!\nAll this blood will never be beautiful\nJust Red!\n\nI, Prometheus,\nAm tiptoeing on the thin, third side of the coin\nThere, I await the fall, my fall.\nWhere by my side, sweet Oceanus?\nSings an old broken song\nEvery day, he sings, a little different broken song than the day before\nBut every day, sings his own broken song.\n\nDear friend,\nI dare not look into your eyes and pick words to utter\nFor they are too vile, unsacred for your heart that already shudders.\nBut the flames that must light dreams\nAre burning them, it seems.\n\nMy children,\nEach one of them shine their own wisps of light\nThe smile on the tip of their lip, so naïve, so bright\nMisread own to fireflies but moth\nJoin the fiercest flame,\nOnly to perish like a cloth.\n\nBut now not, ever again\nAs the early bird might get the worm,\nBut the only bird\nWill get the Sweet Prometheus’ Liver.` },
  { id: "WBH-016", title: "The Falling Lamb", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Mythological / Dark", language: "English", excerpt: "Two crows perch high,\nKnown God, ‘neath the sky,", content: `THE FALLING LAMB\n\nTwo crows perch high,\nKnown God, ‘neath the sky,\nOne waits nigh, oh why\nPerched and shy,\nWith a sigh they lie,\nBy the god ‘neath the sky.\n\nHush, they weep\nOne’s caw, next’s bite\nFlesh out\nBones still\nOn the ground\nHe still lies, ‘neath the sky.\n\nDeath.\n\nTwo crows land low\nLeft with spare,\nFlesh, who did raze?\n‘Ahh!’ The black bird says\n‘Look o’er there,\nThe next fall from yon high’\n‘Ahh!\nThe lamb’ they sigh and wait.` },
  { id: "WBH-017", title: "Tick then Tock", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Existential / Vulnerable", language: "English", excerpt: "You give up\nYou go quiet\nYou quit conflict", content: `TICK THEN TOCK\n\nYou give up\nYou go quiet\nYou quit conflict\nCancel collision\nCall it calm\nWhen it’s only collapse\nYou avoid confrontation.\n\nYou aren’t running\nYou repeat that softly\nYou’re just letting it all happen\nStanding still\nWhile things slip, slide, settle\nInto something smaller\nAfter you discover\nHow many things can go wrong\nLife loosens its grip on living\nAnd leans into waiting\nWaiting rooms\nWaiting rhythms\nWaiting for the next disaster\nLike it’s already scheduled\nSimply slow\n\nThe enlightenment is over\nThe light didn’t leave\nIt dulled, dimmed, degraded\nWhat we live in now\nIs dis-enlightenment\nWe use rebellion\nAs refuge\nWe use criticism\nAs counterfeit participation\nWe speak sharp sentences\nAnd mistake them for movement\n\n“denial”\nIsn’t the right word\nBut it’s the first one\nThat stumbles forward\nInside my skull\nA radar rattles\nDays drilling\nNights kneeling with knives,\nReady for raid and rupture\nAnd i cancel the report.\nI hum along\nTo the sirens’ signal\nSoothing, seductive, sincere\nAs i lie down\nIn the middle of my operation base\nSprawled where strategy should be\nEmotions thin\nFade.\nFlatten into mirage\nHeat without hydration\nFeeling without form\n\nThen the thought starts circling\nSlow, surgical\nNot dramatic—just direct\nWhat if i’m nothing\nBut a brain\nBolted to aluminium\nChemically convinced\nElectrically instructed\nTo believe this is life?\nA program\nProcessing programs\nInside a simulated setting\nAm i an experiment\nThe world a constructed stage\nActors assigned affection\nWeather rendered as effects\nSky painted\nSea stretched like a screen\nAcross a lightbulb horizon?\nIs everything\nA set?\nIs that it?\nCamera, roll–\nAction.\n\nI look at my palms\nMy veins,\nMy body\nAnd say\nNo shit\n\n“trance”\nNot the right word\nBut every breath feels guided\nGoverned\nGently hijacked\n“hypnosis”\nNot the right word\nBut something is counting me\nDown\nWithout disclosure\n“cyborg”\nIsn’t the right word\nBut my nerves feel networked\n“existential”\nIsn’t the right word\nBut it sits heavy\nInsistent\nInescapable\n\nInformation floods\nSynapses stall\nA database of failed signals\nBombards the brain\nAttention feels a clock tick long\n\nTick\nI’m so freaking tired\nTock\nMy chest aches\nPumping red\nRepeating itself without relief\nTick\nOut of cigarettes\nTock\nFind stable work\nTock\nSomething smells scorched\nTick\nConsciousness becomes the enemy\nTock\nWhere is my lighter\nTick\nMy mouth tastes metallic\nTick\nSelf-disgust\nBuzzes, blurs\nPasses behind the iris\nTick\nThen tock\n\n“depression” isn't the right word\nBut that's the only word that comes to mind\nMy little identity paradigm shifts\nPeriod revival\nI reconstruct daily suffering\nNot to cure it\nBut to curate it\nAlign it\nArrange it\nIn the order i want\nHow my dysfunction\nFunctions flawlessly\nTo make order out of chaos\n\nYou collect details, look for a pattern\nAnd organise\nYou make sense out of senseless facts\nThese useless details\nThey're only useless until you connect them all together\nEverything is nothing by itself\nYou puzzle together bits of everything\nYou shuffle and reorganise\nCreate a brief timeline of your collapse\nThe countdown to start your wreckage\nThe oracle of your mistakes\nMisery colosseum\nDevelop a formula and wrap it in coil\nThen put it in a capsule so it wont go extinct because it's all you've ever known\nAnd you are nothing without your pain\n\nA montage\nAssembled\nArchived\nDocumented\nMummified\nYour latest ancient ruins\n\nI say deliver me from hope\nI say deliver me from change\nI say deliver me\nFrom me\n\nPlease don’t fasten your seat belts\nAs we begin\nOur terminal descent\nInto oblivion\n\n“suicide”\nIsn’t the right word\nBut it’s the first one\nThat comes to mind` },
  { id: "WBH-018", title: "To the Girl Sitting at the Table...", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Raw / Hopeful", language: "English", excerpt: "To the girl sitting at the table\nRunning a blade on her ink-stained wrist", content: `TO THE GIRL SITTING AT THE TABLE...\n\nTo the girl sitting at the table\nRunning a blade on her ink-stained wrist\nWondering if red and blue will make purple next morning.\n\nThere will be a night a few years from now,\nWhen one of your closest will be sitting\nOn the precipice of these thoughts\nLegs dangling over several storeys of despair, waiting\nYou will need to tug them back and hold ‘em close\nThey will need your hand.\n\nTo the girl sitting at the table\nWondering a mouthful of pills will cloud the pain\none day,\nA few years from now,\nYou will know that oblivion isn’t the answer,\nLearn that you don’t always have to gulp down your fears,\nAlone.\n\nYou will hum through your pain,\nWatch the maelstrom that is your thoughts\nChange rhythms to match.\nYou will remind others of what it is like\nTo sing with a naked voice.\nYou will need those lips.\n\nTo the girl sitting at the table\nEyes tracing a few words over and over,\nHoping they will rearrange themselves\nTo smell something other than what is.\n\nA few years from now,\nThere will be a time when verses spring out of you\nLike wildflowers, covering every inch\nOf aching heart, ribboned into corsages,\nFor those in need of faith,\nAlways enough to make every person smell like home.\nYou will need those eyes,\nThose words,\nThat heart.\n\nTo the girl sitting at the table,\nYou need to be there,\nA few years from now.\n\nTo the girl sitting at the table,\nGet up.` },
  { id: "WBH-019", title: "You're Enough", collection: "Written by Hand", mood: "Introspective / Vulnerable", language: "English", excerpt: "You always feel delinquent\nBecause of the person you “aren’t”", content: `YOU’RE ENOUGH\n\nYou always feel delinquent\nBecause of the person you “aren’t”\nFor the world has already inscribed a definition\nWherein…\nYou don’t fit so well\nIt’s like trying to be a piece of paradise in a place like hell\nIt’s like caring for the dead and make sure he’s well\nIt’s like facing the blaze after an eternity of chase\nIt’s the state of daze where the state of reciprocity is never balanced.\n\nNo…\nIt’s not that I’m embracing my negative self\nIt’s just that….\nI know that you know how to reach the peak of that hidden shelf\nAnd you’re aware what resides there….\nA light piercing itself out of the realm of darkness\nSomething that makes sure to let you know what you’re facing\nSomething that allows you to see through the wrongs\nSomething…\nThat never declines to accept your real self.\n\nYou wanna reach there…the peak…\nYou can ...\nBut you won’t…\nMaybe you aren’t compatible to make it out\nMaybe coz you don’t know what is it all about\nMaybe it’s something you won’t live without\nMaybe…just maybe…\nYou’ll end up with a rout.\n\nYou try to reach out your hands to the god\nBut fear to shake your own in the very first place\nWhat are the odds?\nYou don’t know yourself.\n\nJust think of it…\nEvery day you sail through sheer jagged thoughts\nAnd cool running dreams\nThe merciless curse of emotions\nOverflows in your exhilarating dreams\nThat witnesses the chaotic times\nOf the dark and ancient old\nWhere the mystifying warrior’s heart of yours\nWas once branded honorable and bold\nBut now,\nOn your shoulders, sits despair\nCrawling through your spine\nTo the maroon of your heart\nWhere the tears sit and whine\nAnd numb you to the mark.\n\nI know this becomes annoying overtime\nAnd makes you think sometimes…\n“Am I self-publicizing things at times?”\nMaybe…I’m not sure\nWhat I’m sure about is\nThere’s an exhaustive introspection\nIn the light behind your eyes\nYet, you stay silent\nIn the wake of another sleepless night.\n\nI’m here from the start\nThough the noisy mind of mine haven’t yet stopped\nBut that’s the interesting part…\nCan you think of something so vast?\nThat lives ahead of past\nSomething so well that crafted\nThat manages the time draft\nAnd lets you live in the presence\nBut leaves you with such an absence\nOf yourself\nThat you can’t even control your actions\n\nAnd eventually you find yourself in a passage\nOf nothingness\nJust something so blind that can see\nBetween the spaces of light\nWhich awakens in the night\nAnd won’t even allow you to erase the sight\nThat you might\nEnd up adore it so much\nThat being so dark\nIt yet feels so bright\n\nYou’re tired now…\nOf all the false promises you make\nHopes of people that you take\nAnd piece by piece\nSo subtly that you break\nWhere the Satan would be confused too\nThat what flake of yours should he take\nAnd keep it in his pocket\nSo well hidden...\nThat you won’t be able to retake it\nAt any stake\nBut it’s not just your mistake.\n\nAt times, you feel sometimes\nThat this was supposed to be\nOf the people by the people and\nFor the people\nBut there seem no people\nInterested in any of the people\nThat are continuously dissected by the people\nWhich reside around our people.\nThe more the people\nIgnore the people\nThat buy the people\nAnd with no remorse divide the people.\n\nBut still,\nI like people\nBecause even when the situation’s dire\nIts only ever people who are able to inspire\nAnd on paper, it’s hard to see how we all cope\nBut in the bottom of the pandora’s box, there’s still hope.\n\nYou know we strive to feel\nand fill\nThe emptiness of the voids in our chest\nWhen we think that we ain’t that blessed\nBut I don’t understand why we behave so rough\nwith ourselves\nAnd never tell the mirror stuck in the shelf\nThat I’m enough.\nWe never say\nThat perfection resides in that reflection\nAnd no matter what happens…\nI’m not gonna cross my own way.\n\nAnd then with time\nWith chime\nYou end up saying\n“I know I exaggerate sometimes\nI’m a storyteller\nOr just a liar\nThere are truths that I hide\nSo well and so wise\nThat I end up telling lies\nSlowly and eventually, I disguise\nAnd enter the eternal loop of disdain to be precise\nAnd live in hoax so large in size\nThat I fail to remember later\nThat at the end of the day all it is\nIs just a lie.”\n\nMy little rose, don’t think about that cave\nThat took your petals away\nAs how much longing must fall like snow\nBefore that one spring day\nSo, lets dress these words in golden chiffon\nLet’s place a mask on their lucid faces\nLet the broth of hell bubble up their throat\nLet the night mother sacrifice her birth\nLet every drop of dew fall upon your leaves\nAnd let, every bone in you, that shivers, believe\nThat you are enough.` },
  
  // Borrowed Ledger Entries
  { id: "BL-001", title: "Browning Decides to be a Poet", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Reflective", language: "English", excerpt: "In these red labyrinths of London\nI find that I have chosen...", content: `Browning Decides to be a Poet — Jorge Luis Borges\n\nIn these red labyrinths of London\nI find that I have chosen\nthe strangest of all callings,\nsave that, in its way, any calling is strange.\n\nLike the alchemist\nwho sought the philosopher's stone\nin quicksilver,\nI shall make everyday words—\nthe gambler's marked cards, the common coin—\ngive off the magic that was theirs\nwhen Thor was both the god and the din,\nthe thunderclap and the prayer.\n\nIn today's dialect\nI shall say, in my fashion, eternal things:\nI shall try to be worthy\nof the great echo of Byron.\n\nThis dust that I am will be invulnerable.\nIf a woman shares my love\nmy verse will touch the tenth sphere of the concentric heavens;\nif a woman turns my love aside\nI will make of my sadness a music,\na full river to resound through time.\n\nI shall live by forgetting myself.\nI shall be the face I glimpse and forget,\nI shall be Judas who takes on\nthe divine mission of being a betrayer,\nI shall be Caliban in his bog,\nI shall be a mercenary who dies\nwithout fear and without faith,\nI shall be Polycrates, who looks in awe\nupon the seal returned by fate.\n\nI will be the friend who hates me.\nThe persian will give me the nightingale, and Rome the sword.\nMasks, agonies, resurrections\nwill weave and unweave my life,\n- and in time I shall be Robert Browning.` },
  { id: "BL-002", title: "Instants", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Reflective", language: "English", excerpt: "If I could live again my life,\nIn the next - I'll try, - to make more mistakes,", content: `Instants — Jorge Luis Borges\n\nIf I could live again my life,\nIn the next - I'll try, - to make more mistakes,\nI won't try to be so perfect,\nI'll be more relaxed,\nI'll be more full - than I am now,\nIn fact, I'll take fewer things seriously,\nI'll be less hygienic,\nI'll take more risks,\nI'll take more trips,\nI'll watch more sunsets,\nI'll climb more mountains,\nI'll swim more rivers,\nI'll go to more places - I've never been,\nI'll eat more ice creams and less (lime) beans,\nI'll have more real problems - and less imaginary ones.\n\nI was one of those people who live\nprudent and prolific lives -\neach minute of his life,\nOf course that I had moments of joy - but,\nif I could go back I'll try to have only good moments,\nIf you don't know - that's what life is made of,\nDon't lose the now!\n\nI was one of those who never goes anywhere\nwithout a thermometer,\nwithout a hot-water bottle,\nand without an umbrella and without a parachute,\nIf I could live again - I will travel light,\nIf I could live again - I'll try to work bare feet\nat the beginning of spring till\nthe end of autumn,\nI'll ride more carts,\nI'll watch more sunrises and play with more children,\nIf I have the life to live - but now I am 85,\nand I know that I am dying ...` },
  { id: "BL-003", title: "Death Sonnet I", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Mournful", language: "English", excerpt: "From the icy niche where men placed you\nI lower your body to the sunny, poor earth.", content: `Death Sonnet I — Gabriela Mistral\n\nFrom the icy niche where men placed you\nI lower your body to the sunny, poor earth.\nThey didn't know I too must sleep in it\nand dream on the same pillow.\n\nI place you in the sunny ground, with a\nmother's sweet care for her napping child,\nand the earth will be a soft cradle\nwhen it receives your hurt childlike body.\n\nI scatter bits of earth and rose dust,\nand in the moon's airy and blue powder\nwhat is left of you is a prisoner.\n\nI leave singing my lovely revenge.\nNo hand will reach into the obscure depth\nto argue with me over your handful of bones.` },
  { id: "BL-004", title: "Poems of the Mothers", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Gentle", language: "English", excerpt: "I was kissed, and I am othered: another,\nbecause of the pulse that echoes the pulse in my veins;", content: `Poems of the Mothers — Gabriela Mistral\n\nI was kissed, and I am othered: another,\nbecause of the pulse that echoes the pulse in my veins;\nanother, because of the breath I feel within my breath.\nMy belly, now, is as noble as my heart ...\n\nAnd now I feel in my own breathing an exhalation of flowers:\nall because of the one who rests inside me gently,\nas the dew on the grass!` },
  { id: "BL-005", title: "Your Laughter", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Joyful", language: "English", excerpt: "Take bread away from me, if you wish,\ntake air away, but\ndo not take from me your laughter.", content: `Your Laughter — Pablo Neruda\n\nTake bread away from me, if you wish,\ntake air away, but\ndo not take from me your laughter.\n\nDo not take away the rose,\nthe lance flower that you pluck,\nthe water that suddenly\nbursts forth in joy,\nthe sudden wave\nof silver born in you.\n\nMy struggle is harsh and I come back\nwith eyes tired\nat times from having seen\nthe unchanging earth,\nbut when your laughter enters\nit rises to the sky seeking me\nand it opens for me all\nthe doors of life.\n\nMy love, in the darkest\nhour your laughter\nopens, and if suddenly\nyou see my blood staining\nthe stones of the street,\nlaugh, because your laughter\nwill be for my hands\nlike a fresh sword.\n\nNext to the sea in the autumn,\nyour laughter must raise\nits foamy cascade,\nand in the spring, love,\nI want your laughter like\nthe flower I was waiting for,\nthe blue flower, the rose\nof my echoing country.\n\nLaugh at the night,\nat the day, at the moon,\nlaugh at the twisted\nstreets of the island,\nlaugh at this clumsy\nboy who loves you,\nbut when I open\nmy eyes and close them,\nwhen my steps go,\nwhen my steps return,\ndeny me bread, air,\nlight, spring,\nbut never your laughter\nfor I would die.` },
  { id: "BL-006", title: "If You Forget Me", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Melancholic", language: "English", excerpt: "I want you to know one thing.\nYou know how this is:", content: `If You Forget Me — Pablo Neruda\n\nI want you to know one thing.\nYou know how this is:\nif I look\nat the crystal moon, at the red branch\nof the slow autumn at my window,\nif I touch\nnear the fire\nthe impalpable ash\nor the wrinkled body of the log,\neverything carries me to you,\nas if everything that exists,\naromas, light, metals,\nwere little boats\nthat sail\ntoward those isles of yours that wait for me.\n\nWell, now,\nif little by little you stop loving me\nI shall stop loving you little by little.\n\nIf suddenly\nyou forget me\ndo not look for me,\nfor I shall already have forgotten you.\n\nIf you think it long and mad,\nthe wind of banners\nthat passes through my life,\nand you decide\nto leave me at the shore\nof the heart where I have roots,\nremember\nthat on that day,\nat that hour,\nI shall lift my arms\nand my roots will set off\nto seek another land.\n\nBut\nif each day,\neach hour,\nyou feel that you are destined for me\nwith implacable sweetness,\nif each day a flower\nclimbs up to your lips to seek me,\nah my love, ah my own,\nin me all that fire is repeated,\nin me nothing is extinguished or forgotten,\nmy love feeds on your love, beloved,\nand as long as you live it will be in your arms\nwithout leaving mine.` },
  { id: "BL-007", title: "To My Brother Miguel in Memoriam", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Melancholic", language: "English", excerpt: "I'm sitting here on the old patio\nbeside your absence. It is a black well.", content: `To My Brother Miguel in Memoriam — Cesar Vallejo\n\nI'm sitting here on the old patio\nbeside your absence. It is a black well.\nWe'd be playing, now...\nI can hear Mama yell\n"Boys! Calm down!" We'd laugh, and off I'd go\nto hide where you'd never look... \nunder the stairs,\nin the hall, the attic...\nThen you'd do the same.\n\nMiguel, we were too good at that game.\nEverything would always end in tears.\nNo one was laughing on that August night\nyou went to hide away again, so late\nit was almost dawn.\n\nBut now your brother's through\nwith this hunting and hunting and never finding you.\nThe shadows crowd him.\n\nMiguel, will you hurry\nand show yourself? Mama will only worry.` },
  { id: "BL-008", title: "Weary Rings", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Weary", language: "English", excerpt: "There are desires to return, to love, to not disappear,\nand there are desires to die, fought by two", content: `Weary Rings — Cesar Vallejo\n\nThere are desires to return, to love, to not disappear,\nand there are desires to die, fought by two\nopposing waters that have never isthmused.\nThere are desires for a great kiss that would shroud Life,\none that ends in the Africa of a fiery agony,\na suicide!\n\nThere are desires to...\nhave no desires, Lord;\nI point my deicidal finger at you:\nthere are desires to not have had a heart.\n\nSpring returns, returns and will depart.\nAnd God,\nbent in time, repeats himself, and passes, passes\nwith the spinal column of the Universe on his back.\n\nWhen my temples beat their lugubrious drum,\nwhen the dream engraved on a dagger aches me,\nthere are desires to be left standing in this verse!` },
  { id: "BL-009", title: "As One Listens to the Rain", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Tranquil", language: "English", excerpt: "Listen to me as one listens to the rain,\nnot attentive, not distracted,", content: `As One Listens to the Rain — Octavio Paz\n\nListen to me as one listens to the rain,\nnot attentive, not distracted,\nlight footsteps, thin drizzle,\nwater that is air, air that is time,\nthe day is still leaving,\nthe night has yet to arrive,\nfigurations of mist\nat the turn of the corner,\nfigurations of time\nat the bend in this pause,\n\nlisten to me as one listens to the rain,\nwithout listening, hear what I say\nwith eyes open inward, asleep\nwith all five senses awake,\nit's raining, light footsteps, a murmur of syllables,\nair and water, words with no weight:\nwhat we are and are,\nthe days and years, this moment,\nweightless time and heavy sorrow,\n\nlisten to me as one listens to the rain,\nwet asphalt is shining,\nsteam rises and walks away,\nnight unfolds and looks at me,\nyou are you and your body of steam,\nyou and your face of night,\nyou and your hair, unhurried lightning,\nyou cross the street and enter my forehead,\nfootsteps of water across my eyes,\nlisten to me as one listens to the rain.` },
  { id: "BL-010", title: "The Street", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Mysterious", language: "English", excerpt: "Here is a long and silent street.\nI walk in blackness and I stumble and fall", content: `The Street — Octavio Paz\n\nHere is a long and silent street.\nI walk in blackness and I stumble and fall\nand rise, and I walk blind, my feet\ntrampling the silent stones and the dry leaves.\nSomeone behind me also tramples, stones, leaves:\nif I slow down, he slows;\nif I run, he runs\nI turn:\nnobody.\n\nEverything dark and doorless,\nonly my steps aware of me,\nI turning and turning among these corners\nwhich lead forever to the street\nwhere nobody waits for, nobody follows me,\nwhere I pursue a man who stumbles\nand rises and says when he sees me:\nnobody.` },
  { id: "BL-011", title: "I Felt a Funeral in my Brain", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Psychological", language: "English", excerpt: "I felt a Funeral, in my Brain,\nAnd Mourners to and fro", content: `I Felt a Funeral in my Brain — Emily Dickinson\n\nI felt a Funeral, in my Brain,\nAnd Mourners to and fro\nKept treading - treading - till it seemed\nThat Sense was breaking through -\n\nAnd when they all were seated,\nA Service, like a Drum -\nKept beating - beating - till I thought\nMy mind was going numb -\n\nAnd then I heard them lift a Box\nAnd creak across my Soul\nWith those same Boots of Lead, again,\nThen Space - began to toll,\n\nAs all the Heavens were a Bell,\nAnd Being, but an Ear,\nAnd I, and Silence, some strange Race,\nWrecked, solitary, here -\n\nAnd then a Plank in Reason, broke,\nAnd I dropped down, and down -\nAnd hit a World, at every plunge,\nAnd Finished knowing - then -` },
  { id: "BL-012", title: "A Noiseless Patient Spider", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Contemplative", language: "English", excerpt: "A noiseless patient spider,\nI mark’d where on a little promontory it stood isolated,", content: `A Noiseless Patient Spider — Walt Whitman\n\nA noiseless patient spider,\nI mark’d where on a little promontory it stood isolated,\nMark’d how to explore the vacant vast surrounding,\nIt launch’d forth filament, filament, filament, out of itself,\nEver unreeling them, ever tirelessly speeding them.\n\nAnd you O my soul where you stand,\nSurrounded, detached, in measureless oceans of space,\nCeaselessly musing, venturing, throwing, seeking the spheres to connect them,\nTill the bridge you will need be form’d, till the ductile anchor hold,\nTill the gossamer thread you fling catch somewhere, O my soul.` },
  { id: "BL-013", title: "Brahma", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Philosophical", language: "English", excerpt: "If the red slayer think he slays,\nOr if the slain think he is slain,", content: `Brahma — Ralph Waldo Emerson\n\nIf the red slayer think he slays,\nOr if the slain think he is slain,\nThey know not well the subtle ways\nI keep, and pass, and turn again.\n\nFar or forgot to me is near;\nShadow and sunlight are the same;\nThe vanished gods to me appear;\nAnd one to me are shame and fame.\n\nThey reckon ill who leave me out;\nWhen me they fly, I am the wings;\nI am the doubter and the doubt,\nI am the hymn the Brahmin sings.\n\nThe strong gods pine for my abode,\nAnd pine in vain the sacred Seven;\nBut thou, meek lover of the good!\nFind me, and turn thy back on heaven.` },
  { id: "BL-014", title: "Birches", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Nostalgic", language: "English", excerpt: "When I see birches bend to left and right\nAcross the lines of straighter darker trees,", content: `Birches — Robert Frost\n\nWhen I see birches bend to left and right\nAcross the lines of straighter darker trees,\nI like to think some boy’s been swinging them.\nBut swinging doesn’t bend them down to stay\nAs ice-storms do. Often you must have seen them\nLoaded with ice a sunny winter morning\nAfter a rain. They click upon themselves\nAs the breeze rises, and turn many-colored\nAs the stir cracks and crazes their enamel.\n\nSoon the sun’s warmth makes them shed crystal shells\nShattering and avalanching on the snow-crust—\nSuch heaps of broken glass to sweep away\nYou'd think the inner dome of heaven had fallen.\nThey are dragged to the withered bracken by the load,\nAnd they seem not to break; though once they are bowed\nSo low for long, they never right themselves:\nYou may see their trunks arching in the woods\nYears afterwards, trailing their leaves on the ground\nLike girls on hands and knees that throw their hair\nBefore them over their heads to dry in the sun.\n\nBut I was going to say when Truth broke in\nWith all her matter-of-fact about the ice-storm\nI should prefer to have some boy bend them\nAs he went out and in to fetch the cows—\nSome boy too far from town to learn baseball,\nWhose only play was what he found himself,\nSummer or winter, and could play alone.\n\nOne by one he subdued his father's trees\nBy riding them down over and over again\nUntil he took the stiffness out of them,\nAnd not one but hung limp, not one was left\nFor him to conquer. He learned all there was\nTo learn about not launching out too soon\nAnd so not carrying the tree away\nClear to the ground. He always kept his poise\nTo the top branches, climbing carefully\nWith the same pains you use to fill a cup\nUp to the brim, and even above the brim.\nThen he flung outward, feet first, with a swish,\nKicking his way down through the air to the ground.\n\nSo was I once myself a swinger of birches.\nAnd so I dream of going back to be.\nIt’s when I’m weary of considerations,\nAnd life is too much like a pathless wood\nWhere your face burns and tickles with the cobwebs\nBroken across it, and one eye is weeping\nFrom a twig’s having lashed across it open.\n\nI'd like to get away from earth awhile\nAnd then come back to it and begin over.\nMay no fate willfully misunderstand me\nAnd half grant what I wish and snatch me away\nNot to return. Earth’s the right place for love:\nI don’t know where it's likely to go better.\nI'd like to go by climbing a birch tree,\nAnd climb black branches up a snow-white trunk\nToward heaven, till the tree could bear no more,\nBut dipped its top and set me down again.\nThat would be good both going and coming back.\nOne could do worse than be a swinger of birches.` },
  { id: "BL-015", title: "The Sparrow", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Reflective", language: "English", excerpt: "A little bird, with plumage brown,\nBeside my window flutters down,", content: `The Sparrow — Paul Laurence Dunbar\n\nA little bird, with plumage brown,\nBeside my window flutters down,\nA moment chirps its little strain,\nTen taps upon my window–pane,\nAnd chirps again, and hops along,\nTo call my notice to its song;\nBut I work on, nor heed its lay,\nTill, in neglect, it flies away.\n\nSo birds of peace and hope and love\nCome fluttering earthward from above,\nTo settle on life’s window–sills,\nAnd ease our load of earthly ills;\nBut we, in traffic’s rush and din\nToo deep engaged to let them in,\nWith deadened heart and sense plod on,\nNor know our loss till they are gone.` },
  { id: "BL-016", title: "The Red Wheelbarrow", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Minimalist", language: "English", excerpt: "so much depends upon\na red wheel barrow", content: `The Red Wheelbarrow — William Carlos Williams\n\nso much depends upon\na red wheel barrow\n\nglazed with rain water\n\nbeside the white chickens` },
  { id: "BL-017", title: "Thirteen Ways of Looking at a Blackbird", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Abstract", language: "English", excerpt: "Among twenty snowy mountains,\nThe only moving thing", content: `Thirteen Ways of Looking at a Blackbird — Wallace Stevens\n\nI\nAmong twenty snowy mountains,\nThe only moving thing\nWas the eye of the blackbird.\n\nII\nI was of three minds,\nLike a tree\nIn which there are three blackbirds.\n\nIII\nThe blackbird whirled in the autumn winds.\nIt was a small part of the pantomime.\n\nIV\nA man and a woman\nAre one.\nA man and a woman and a blackbird\nAre one.\n\nV\nI do not know which to prefer,\nThe beauty of inflections\nOr the beauty of innuendoes,\nThe blackbird whistling\nOr just after.\n\nVI\nIcicles filled the long window\nWith barbaric glass.\nThe shadow of the blackbird\nCrossed it, to and fro.\nThe mood\nTraced in the shadow\nAn indecipherable cause.\n\nVII\nO thin men of Haddam,\nWhy do you imagine golden birds?\nDo you not see how the blackbird\nWalks around the feet\nOf the women about you?\n\nVIII\nI know noble accents\nAnd lucid, inescapable rhythms;\nBut I know, too,\nThat the blackbird is involved\nIn what I know.\n\nIX\nWhen the blackbird flew out of sight,\nIt marked the edge\nOf one of many circles.\n\nX\nAt the sight of blackbirds\nFlying in a green light,\nEven the bawds of euphony\nWould cry out sharply.\n\nXI\nHe rode over Connecticut\nIn a glass coach.\nOnce, a fear pierced him,\nIn that he mistook\nThe shadow of his equipage\nFor blackbirds.\n\nXII\nThe river is moving.\nThe blackbird must be flying.\n\nXIII\nIt was evening all afternoon.\nIt was snowing\nAnd it was going to snow.\nThe blackbird sat\nIn the cedar-limbs.` },
  { id: "BL-018", title: "Daddy", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Dark", language: "English", excerpt: "You do not do, you do not do\nAny more, black shoe", content: `Daddy — Sylvia Plath\n\nYou do not do, you do not do\nAny more, black shoe\nIn which I have lived like a foot\nFor thirty years, poor and white,\nBarely daring to breathe or Achoo.\n\nDaddy, I have had to kill you.\nYou died before I had time——\nMarble-heavy, a bag full of God,\nGhastly statue with one gray toe\nBig as a Frisco seal\n\nAnd a head in the freakish Atlantic\nWhere it pours bean green over blue\nIn the waters off beautiful Nauset.\nI used to pray to recover you.\nAch, du.\n\nIn the German tongue, in the Polish town\nScraped flat by the roller\nOf wars, wars, wars.\nBut the name of the town is common.\nMy Polack friend\n\nSays there are a dozen or two.\nSo I never could tell where you\nPut your foot, your root,\nI never could talk to you.\nThe tongue stuck in my jaw.\n\nIt stuck in a barb wire snare.\nIch, ich, ich, ich,\nI could hardly speak.\nI thought every German was you.\nAnd the language obscene\n\nAn engine, an engine\nChuffing me off like a Jew.\nA Jew to Dachau, Auschwitz, Belsen.\nI began to talk like a Jew.\nI think I may well be a Jew.\n\nThe snows of the Tyrol, the clear beer of Vienna\nAre not very pure or true.\nWith my gipsy ancestress and my weird luck\nAnd my Taroc pack and my Taroc pack\nI may be a bit of a Jew.\n\nI have always been scared of you,\nWith your Luftwaffe, your gobbledygoo.\nAnd your neat mustache\nAnd your Aryan eye, bright blue.\nPanzer-man, panzer-man, O You——\n\nNot God but a swastika\nSo black no sky could squeak through.\nEvery woman adores a Fascist,\nThe boot in the face, the brute\nBrute heart of a brute like you.\n\nYou stand at the blackboard, daddy,\nIn the picture I have of you,\nA cleft in your chin instead of your foot\nBut no less a devil for that, no not\nAny less the black man who\n\nBit my pretty red heart in two.\nI was ten when they buried you.\nAt twenty I tried to die\nAnd get back, back, back to you.\nI thought even the bones would do.\n\nBut they pulled me out of the sack,\nAnd they stuck me together with glue.\nAnd then I knew what to do.\nI made a model of you,\nA man in black with a Meinkampf look\n\nAnd a love of the rack and the screw.\nAnd I said I do, I do.\nSo daddy, I’m finally through.\nThe black telephone’s off at the root,\nThe voices just can’t worm through.\n\nIf I’ve killed one man, I’ve killed two——\nThe vampire who said he was you\nAnd drank my blood for a year,\nSeven years, if you want to know.\nDaddy, you can lie back now.\n\nThere’s a stake in your fat black heart\nAnd the villagers never liked you.\nThey are dancing and stamping on you.\nThey always knew it was you.\nDaddy, daddy, you bastard, I’m through.` },
  { id: "BL-019", title: "I, Too", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Empowerment", language: "English", excerpt: "I, too, sing America.\nI am the darker brother.", content: `I, Too — Langston Hughes\n\nI, too, sing America.\n\nI am the darker brother.\nThey send me to eat in the kitchen\nWhen company comes,\nBut I laugh,\nAnd eat well,\nAnd grow strong.\n\nTomorrow,\nI’ll be at the table\nWhen company comes.\nNobody’ll dare\nSay to me,\n“Eat in the kitchen,”\nThen.\n\nBesides,\nThey’ll see how beautiful I am\nAnd be ashamed—\n\nI, too, am America.` },
  { id: "BL-020", title: "Patriot", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Satirical", language: "English", excerpt: "I am standing for peace and non-violence. Why world is fighting fighting", content: `Patriot — Nissim Ezekiel\n\nI am standing for peace and non-violence.\nWhy world is fighting fighting\nWhy all people of world\nAre not following Mahatma Gandhi,\nI am simply not understanding.\nAncient Indian Wisdom is 100% correct,\nI should say even 200% correct,\nBut modern generation is neglecting -\nToo much going for fashion and foreign thing.\nOther day I'm reading newspaper\n(Every day I'm reading Times of India\nTo improve my English Language)\nHow one goonda fellow\nThrew stone at Indirabehn.\nMust be student unrest fellow, I am thinking.\nFriends, Romans, Countrymen, I am saying (to myself)\nLend me the ears.\nEverything is coming -\nRegeneration, Remuneration, Contraception.\nBe patiently, brothers and sisters.\n\nYou want one glass lassi?\nVery good for digestion.\nWith little salt, lovely drink,\nBetter than wine;\nNot that I am ever tasting the wine.\nI'm the total teetotaller, completely total,\nBut I say\nWine is for the drunkards only.\n\nWhat you think of prospects of world peace?\nPakistan behaving like this,\nChina behaving like that,\nIt is making me really sad, I am telling you.\nReally, most harassing me.\nAll men are brothers, no?\nIn India also\nGujaratis, Maharatrians, Hindiwallahs\nAll brothers -\nThough some are having funny habits.\nStill, you tolerate me,\nI tolerate you,\nOne day Ram Rajya is surely coming.\n\nYou are going?\nBut you will visit again\nAny time, any day,\nI am not believing in ceremony\nAlways I am enjoying your company.` },
  { id: "BL-021", title: "Postcard from Kashmir", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Melancholic", language: "English", excerpt: "Kashmir shrinks into my mailbox, my home a neat four by six inches.", content: `Postcard from Kashmir — Agha Shahid Ali\n\nKashmir shrinks into my mailbox,\nmy home a neat four by six inches.\n\nI always loved neatness. Now I hold\nthe half-inch Himalayas in my hand.\n\nThis is home. And this the closest\nI'll ever be to home. When I return,\nthe colors won't be so brilliant,\nthe Jhelum's waters so clean,\nso ultramarine. My love\nso overexposed.\n\nAnd my memory will be a little\nout of focus, in it\na giant negative, black\nand white, still undeveloped.` },
  { id: "BL-022", title: "Dawn at Puri", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Somber", language: "English", excerpt: "Endless crow noises\nA skull in the holy sands", content: `Dawn at Puri — Jayanta Mahapatra\n\nEndless crow noises\nA skull in the holy sands\ntilts its empty country towards hunger.\n\nWhite-clad widowed Women\npast the centers of their lives\nare waiting to enter the Great Temple\n\nTheir austere eyes\nstare like those caught in a net\nhanging by the dawn's shining strands of faith.\n\nThe fail early light catches\nruined, leprous shells leaning against one another,\na mass of crouched faces without names,\n\nand suddenly breaks out of my hide\ninto the smoky blaze of a sullen solitary pyre\nthat fills my aging mother:\n\nher last wish to be cremated here\ntwisting uncertainly like light\non the shifting sands.` },
  { id: "BL-023", title: "The Old Playhouse", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Exhausted", language: "English", excerpt: "You planned to tame a swallow, to hold her\nIn the long summer of your love", content: `The Old Playhouse — Kamala Das\n\nYou planned to tame a swallow, to hold her\nIn the long summer of your love so that she would forget\nNot the raw seasons alone, and the homes left behind, but\nAlso her nature, the urge to fly, and the endless\nPathways of the sky.\n\nIt was not to gather knowledge\nOf yet another man that I came to you but to learn\nWhat I was, and by learning, to learn to grow,\nbut every Lesson you gave was about yourself.\nYou were pleased\nWith my body's response, its weather, its usual shallow\nConvulsions.\n\nYou dribbled spittle into my mouth, you poured\nYourself into every nook and cranny, you embalmed\nMy poor lust with your bitter-sweet juices.\nYou called me wife, I was taught to break saccharine into your tea and\nTo offer at the right moment the vitamins.\nCowering Beneath your monstrous ego I ate the magic loaf and\nBecame a dwarf.\nI lost my will and reason, to all your\nQuestions I mumbled incoherent replies.\n\nThe summer begins to pall.\nI remember the rudder breezes\nOf the fall and the smoke from the burning leaves.\nYour room is Always lit by artificial lights, your windows always\nShut.\nEven the air-conditioner helps so little,\nAll pervasive is the male scent of your breath.\nThe cut flowers In the vases have begun to smell of human sweat.\n\nThere is No more singing, no more dance, my mind is an old\nPlayhouse with all its lights put out.\nThe strong man's technique is Always the same, he serves his love in lethal doses,\nFor, love is Narcissus at the water's edge, haunted\nBy its own lonely face, and yet it must seek at last\nAn end, a pure, total freedom, it must will the mirrors\nTo shatter and the kind night to erase the water.` },
  { id: "BL-024", title: "Nani", collection: "Borrowed Ledger", mood: "Memory", language: "English", excerpt: "Nani the pregnant maid hanged herself\nIn the privy one day.", content: `Nani — Kamla Das\n\nNani the pregnant maid hanged herself\nIn the privy one day.\nFor three long hours\nUntil the police came, she was hanging there,\nA clumsy puppet, and when\nthe wind blew\nTurning her gently on the rope, it seemed\nTo us who were children then, that Nani\nWas doing, to delight us, a comic Dance.\n\nThe shrubs grew fast.\nBefore the summer's end\nThe yellow flowers had hugged the doorway\nAnd the walls.\nThe privy, so abandoned,\nBecame an altar then, a sunny shrine\nFor a goddess who was dead.\n\nAnother Year or two, and I asked my\ngrandmother\nOne day, don't you remember Nani, the dark\nPlump one who bathed me near the\nwell?\nGrandmother\nShifted the reading glasses on her nose\nAnd stared at me.\nNani, she asked,\nwho is she?\nWith that question ended Nani.\n\nEach truth\nEnds thus with a query.\nIt is this\ndesigned Deafness that turns mortality into\nImmortality, the definite into\nThe soft indefinite.\n\nThey are lucky\nWho ask questions and move on before\nThe answers come, those wise ones who\nreside\nIn a blue silent zone, unscratched by doubts` },
  { id: "RES-001", title: "Simulacra and Simulation", collection: "Research", mood: "Philosophical", language: "English", excerpt: "The simulacrum is never that which conceals the truth—it is the truth which conceals that there is none.", content: "", pdfUrl: "https://dn720006.ca.archive.org/0/items/baudrillard.-1970.-the-consumer-society/Baudrillard.1981.Simulacra-and-Simulation.pdf" }
];

